<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="Cycle racing in the 1980s: Hinault and LeMond, snow on the Gavia, aerodynamics, clipless pedals, indexed shifting and a more scientific sport."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/periods/1980-1989/teknisk-brydningstid-illustration.webp"/><title>When the bicycle became a laboratory · 1980–1989 · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="periods" danishPath="/perioder/1980-1989/"/><main><article class="wildwest-page decade1970-page">
<header class="wildwest-hero decade1940-hero"><div><p class="section-label">The period 1980–1989</p><h1>When the bicycle became<br/><em>a laboratory</em></h1></div><p>The steel frame remained strong. Around it appeared clipless pedals, disc wheels, indexed gears and aero bars. Not every idea became a lasting solution.</p></header>
<figure class="decade1970-hero-image"><img src={`${base}/images/periods/1980-1989/teknisk-brydningstid-illustration.webp`} alt="Illustration of riders and mechanics with steel bicycles and an experimental time-trial bicycle in the late 1980s"/><figcaption>The steel bicycle beside the decade’s new aerodynamic experiments. AI-generated illustration typical of the period — not an image of a specific race or rider.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="wildwest-lead decade1940-lead"><p class="section-label">A lever on the down tube</p><p>A rider could begin the decade with toe straps and friction shifting and finish it with clipless pedals, indexed gears and aero bars. Change arrived part by part, often in the middle of a race.</p></section>
<section class="era-facts decade1970-facts" aria-label="Highlights from cycle racing in the 1980s">{facts.map(([n,l])=><div><strong>{n}</strong><span>{l}</span></div>)}</section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">New balances of power</p><h2>From Hinault to LeMond</h2></div><div><p>Bernard Hinault dominated the first half of the decade. He won the Tour de France in 1981, 1982 and 1985 and the Giro d’Italia in 1980, 1982 and 1985. He rode as the patron of the peloton and also intervened beyond the results. During Paris–Nice in 1984, a confrontation with demonstrators ended in a scuffle. That is a better story than another list of victories.</p><p>La Vie Claire later brought Hinault and Greg LeMond together on the same team. In 1986, LeMond won the Tour ahead of Hinault and became its first American winner. Three years later, he closed the decade with the smallest winning margin in Tour history: eight seconds ahead of Laurent Fignon.</p><p>Fignon began the final time trial in 1989 with a 50-second lead. LeMond used aero bars and a more compact aerodynamic position. The bars do not explain the result on their own, but they made the technical difference visible all the way onto the Champs-Élysées.</p></div></section>
<section class="decade1970-mechanics"><div><p class="section-label">Aerodynamics is allowed to decide</p><h2>Small front wheels and solid discs</h2><p>Francesco Moser’s hour records in Mexico City in January 1984 made disc wheels and a radical riding position subjects of discussion far beyond the track. Time-trial and track bicycles gained sloping top tubes, small front wheels and a very low front end. They were called <em>funny bikes</em> because their proportions looked wrong after decades of level top tubes and two equally sized wheels.</p><p>The experiments showed that aerodynamic drag could matter more than a few grams. They also created a whole family of solutions that later rules either restricted or made redundant.</p></div><aside aria-label="Technical features of the 1980s"><span>Steel frames</span><span>Indexed shifting</span><span>LOOK pedals</span><span>Disc wheels</span><span>Aero bars</span><span>Small front wheels</span></aside></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy dark"><div><p class="section-label">Feet and fingers</p><h2>The pedal clicks</h2></div><div><p>LOOK transferred the principle of the ski binding to the bicycle and developed the PP65 pedal in 1984. Instead of a toe clip and leather strap, the rider pressed a cleat into a spring-loaded mechanism. The foot could be released by turning the heel outwards.</p><p>Bernard Hinault won the 1985 Tour on the new pedals. It gave the solution a demonstration no catalogue could match. Toe straps did not disappear that day, but the direction was clear.</p><p>At the same time, Shimano SIS made fixed clicks in gear shifting commonplace. The levers remained on the down tube, but the rider no longer had to feel his way to each sprocket. Integrated brake and gear levers only arrived in the early 1990s.</p></div></section>
<section class="decade1940-return"><div><span>Gavia · 1988</span><h2>Summer clothing in snow</h2><p>On stage 14 of the Giro, rain turned to snow near the summit. Johan van der Velde crossed first but had to seek shelter on the descent and lost 47 minutes. Erik Breukink won the stage, while Andy Hampsten took the pink jersey and later the Giro.</p></div><div><span>Denmark · 1983</span><h2>The first yellow jersey</h2><p>Kim Andersen became the first Dane to wear the Tour’s yellow jersey. The decade later gave Danish cycling several more visible moments — among them Jesper Skibby’s encounter with the race car on the Koppenberg in 1987.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy rust"><div><p class="section-label">Food and measurement</p><h2>The body moved closer to the laboratory</h2></div><div><p>Pasta, rice and carbohydrate-rich drinks acquired a clearer place around races. Teams worked more systematically with fluids and energy, while commercial powders and energy products became more common. The old meal of meat, wine and experience did not disappear from every hotel at once.</p><p>The first wireless heart-rate monitors made it possible to follow the heart during training without an entire laboratory. Together with tests of oxygen uptake and workload thresholds, they gave coaches more figures around which to plan.</p><p>The figures brought better training, but also the idea that almost everything could be optimised. That thought did not stop at pasta and pulse.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">The hidden development</p><h2>Blood, cortisone and EPO</h2></div><div><p>Blood transfusions were used in international elite sport in the 1980s. The method increased the quantity of red blood cells and was difficult to detect with the testing available at the time. It was prohibited by the IOC in 1985, but a ban was not the same as a usable test.</p><p>Corticosteroids and other substances also formed part of the sport’s medical grey areas. Documentation varies for individual teams and riders, and a period page should not make the entire peloton identical.</p><p>Recombinant EPO was approved as a medicine at the end of the decade. Its later role in cycling is well documented. Precisely when and how systematically it entered professional cycling is harder to establish. The EPO story therefore begins here as a shadow, not as a claimed explanation for the decade’s speeds.</p></div></section>
<section class="period-feature decade1970-related"><div><p class="section-label">Stories and bicycles</p><h2>The decade close up</h2><p>The Koppenberg shows what happens when a narrow cobbled road meets a modern support-car system. Koga-Miyata shows the same decade’s aerodynamic experiments in metal.</p></div><div class="period-feature-links"><a href={`${base}/en/stories/skibby-koppenberg-1987/`}><span>Story · 1987</span><strong>When the race car ran over Skibby’s bicycle</strong><b>Read the story →</b></a><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/koga-miyata-gentsracer-aero/`}><span>Bicycle · 1983 model</span><strong>Koga-Miyata Gentsracer-Aero</strong><b>View →</b></a></div></section>
<section class="period-sources"><p class="section-label">Sources and image note</p><h2>The basis for this period</h2><ul><li><a href="https://www.letour.fr/en/news/2024/finish-with-a-capital-f-champs-elysees-welcome-to-sprinter-heaven-iv-iv/1316968">Tour de France: LeMond, Fignon and the eight seconds in 1989</a></li><li><a href="https://www.giroditalia.it/en/gavia/">Giro d’Italia: Passo Gavia and the snow stage in 1988</a></li><li><a href="https://www.lookcycle.com/pa-en/inside/about-look-heritage">LOOK: the PP65 pedal and Hinault in 1985</a></li><li><a href="https://library.olympics.com/default/digitalCollection/DigitalCollectionAttachmentDownloadHandler.ashx?documentId=157513&skipWatermark=true">IOC: historical research into blood doping and EPO</a></li></ul><p>The submitted photographs and route maps were used as research references but not published because usage rights are not documented. The two colour photographs from the 1987 Tour also carry visible Shutterstock watermarks.</p></section>
